```
=== DIAGNOSIS ===
is_anomaly: true
faulty_devices: leaf_router_0_0
diagnosis_text:
The BGP daemon (bgpd) on leaf_router_0_0 is not running. The on-disk
/etc/frr/frr.conf is fully correct — it defines AS 65200, router-id
172.16.0.5, two eBGP neighbors (172.16.0.4 → spine_router_0_0 AS 65100,
172.16.0.8 → spine_router_0_1 AS 65101), and the network statement for
10.0.0.0/24. However, because bgpd crashed or was never started, no BGP
sessions were ever established from this leaf. As a result, 10.0.0.0/24
(pc_0_0's subnet) is never advertised into the fabric, and none of the
upstream routers — neither spine nor super-spine — carry a return path to it.
Simultaneously, leaf_router_0_0 itself has learned no BGP routes, so when
pc_0_0 sends traffic toward 10.0.1.0/24, leaf_router_0_0 has no matching
route and immediately generates ICMP Destination Net Unreachable back to the
source. The team's suspicion about spine_router_0_0 is incorrect: that spine's
BGP process is healthy with two of its three sessions fully established; its
session toward 172.16.0.5 is stuck in Active state only because leaf_router_0_0
never opens a TCP connection. The root cause is that bgpd is not running on
leaf_router_0_0. The corrective fix is to restart the FRR service on
leaf_router_0_0 (e.g., service frr restart or systemctl restart frr), which
will reload the existing correct frr.conf, bring up both eBGP sessions to the
spines, advertise 10.0.0.0/24, and restore full fabric reachability between
pc_0_0 and pc_0_1.
=== END ===
